iterator*)An iterator that yields values of type T.:Ž
double_ended*zAn iterator that can also yield from its end.

This is what makes `rev`, `last`, and `rfind` linear rather than quadratic.:F

exact_size*4An iterator whose remaining length is known exactly.:•
into_iterator*ÿA type that can be converted into an iterator.

`for` does not require this: it resolves iterators structurally, so a type
with a suitable `next` is already usable in a loop. This trait is for the
types where iterating means handing back something *else*.:„
	from_iter*sA collection that can be built from an iterator.

Implement this to make a type available as a target of `collect`.:à
collect*ÐCollects an iterator into any collection that implements `from_iter`.

The collection is chosen by the type expected at the call site:

    let names: list[str] = words.collect()

and given explicitly where no expected type exists:

    words.collect[list[str]]()

A `C` that is not a collection of the iterator's elements is rejected at
the call, by the `C: from_iter[T]` bound. `C` comes before `T` so that the
one bracket argument of `words.collect[list[str]]()` names the collection.
Type Params:
  I: the iterator's type; must implement `iterator[T]`.
  C: the collection to build; must implement `from_iter[T]`.
  T: the element type.
Args:
  it: the iterator to drain (moved).
Returns:
  the collected collection.:I
	list_iter*8An iterator over shared references to a list's elements.:N
list_iter_mut*9An iterator over mutable references to a list's elements.:B
